\section{Introduction}
%=============================================================================

This section presents the methodology for evaluating Central Composite Designs (CCDs) using scaled regularised alphabetic optimality criteria. The scaled regularisation approach addresses the limitation of unscaled regularisation, where efficiency values become artificially inflated at high dimensions due to the regularisation parameter dominating near-zero eigenvalues.

%=============================================================================
\section{Limitation of Unscaled Regularisation}
%=============================================================================

\subsection{The Inflation Problem}

For the unscaled regularised information matrix:
\begin{equation}
M_\lambda = X'X + \lambda I_p
\label{eq:unscaled}
\end{equation}

When $X'X$ is singular or near-singular (for $k \geq 7$), many eigenvalues $\mu_i \approx 0$. The regularised eigenvalues become:
\begin{equation}
\mu_i(M_\lambda) = \mu_i(X'X) + \lambda \approx \lambda
\end{equation}

\subsection{Effect on A-Optimality}

The A-criterion becomes:
\begin{equation}
\tr(M_\lambda^{-1}) = \sum_{i=1}^{p} \frac{1}{\mu_i + \lambda} \approx \sum_{i: \mu_i \approx 0} \frac{1}{\lambda} = \frac{p_0}{\lambda}
\end{equation}

where $p_0$ is the number of near-zero eigenvalues.

The A-efficiency:
\begin{equation}
A_{\text{eff},\lambda} = \frac{100p}{N \cdot \tr(M_\lambda^{-1})} \approx \frac{100p}{N \cdot p_0/\lambda} = \frac{100p\lambda}{N \cdot p_0}
\end{equation}

As $p_0$ increases with dimension, the efficiency becomes dominated by $\lambda$ rather than the design structure, leading to artificially high values approaching 100\%.

\subsection{Effect on G-Optimality}

Similarly, the maximum scaled prediction variance:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^2_{\max,\lambda} = \max_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{R}} \mathbf{f}'(\mathbf{x}) M_\lambda^{-1} \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x})
\end{equation}

becomes dominated by $1/\lambda$ terms, causing G-efficiency to inflate.

\subsection{Observed Behaviour}

Table \ref{tab:inflation} demonstrates the inflation problem with unscaled regularisation ($\lambda = 0.01$).

